lls all year show Becerra ahead by double digits, mostly 18-25 points, including an R-sponsored survey at +18", "All seven expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic; markets price a Becerra win near 95%", "Hilton holds a modest fundraising and cash-on-hand edge, but it is far too small to overcome the structural deficit"], "rationale": "Becerra has led every 2026 poll by at least 10 points in a state that leans 12 points Democratic, and unanimous expert ratings plus markets near 95% leave little doubt about the outcome. Hilton's slight money advantage and aggressive ad campaign are the only Republican bright spots, and neither changes the fundamental math.", "watch": "A major late Becerra stumble or an unusually strong anti-Democratic turnout wave could narrow the margin, though it would take a historic shift to change the winner.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Gavin Newsom (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "California's open governor's race pits Democrat Xavier Becerra against Republican Steve Hilton, with the seat vacated by term-limited Gavin Newsom. The state's D+12 partisan lean gives Becerra a strong structural advantage, and polls throughout 2026 have consistently shown him ahead by double digits, with most recent surveys in the 18-to-25-point range. Even a poll sponsored by a Republican-leaning group in early October showed an 18-point Becerra lead.\n\nEvery major race rater calls California Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price a Becerra win around 95 percent. Hilton has slightly outraised Becerra and has run an energetic campaign built on cheeky ads and attacks on Democratic governance, and one late poll showed a narrower 10-point contest. But nothing in the polling, ratings, or money suggests a fundamental shift; the main question is the size of Becerra's victory, not the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9963, "margin": 17.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0},